{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/method-rules.md; sed -n '1,240p' docs/counsel-rulings.md; sed -n '1,240p' docs/r3-rulings.md; sed -n '1,240p' docs/storage-ruling.md; sed -n '1,160p' attest/records/360/build-02.json; sed -n '1,200p' attavo.toml; sed -n '1,160p' /var/folders/k6/kwlls81j5gvf1p1_hbh9mn8m0000gn/T/tmp3644wusr/checks.json; sed -n '1,160p' /var/folders/k6/kwlls81j5gvf1p1_hbh9mn8m0000gn/T/tmp3644wusr/graph.txt\"","aggregated_output":"# Method rules: reads that lie, and what a session owes before it writes\n\nCarried from the pilot's document index on 2026-09-03, stripped of the pilot's specimens. These are the rules the reviewing session kept as habit and broke when tired; in Attest they are the seed of the challenge's answer contract (Phase 3): an answer that does not satisfy them is refused by a script, not by memory. Each rule was earned by at least one specimen in the pilot.\n\n**The one rule that covers all of them: a read taken while something is in flight is stale by construction.** Report it as a timestamped observation, never as state.\n\n1. **Directory listings are served from a cache.**\n2. **A read taken while a session is mid-command** describes a moment that has passed.\n3. **GitHub's API is eventually consistent.**\n4. **A truncated listing is not evidence of absence.**\n5. **A file transfer tool's success message is not delivery.**\n6. **A ruling recorded in a project doc is not repository state.**\n7. **Local `main` is stale after any merge until the operator pulls.** Branch from `origin/main`. An aborted pull leaves it stale for the rest of the session; a three-dot diff against it then attributes another lane's merged work to the branch under review.\n8. **A lane branch's internal commits are checked by nobody.** The PR's checks are the truth.\n9. **An operator-side project doc is not a citable source for an append-only repo record.** **Nor is a live URL.**\n10. **Branch currency is verified at review time, never asserted from memory.**\n11. **A required status check satisfied by \"skipped\" is not a check that ran.**\n12. **A failed `git checkout` leaves the next command acting on the wrong ref.**\n13. **The absence of a subcommand is not evidence the capability is missing.** Search the repository's scripts, commands, workflows and templates first.\n14. **A red check may be an observation about the environment, not a finding about the branch.**\n15. **An aggregated API row is not attribution.**\n16. **A number without its counting method is not reproducible.**\n17. **A local run of a check and CI's run of it are different programs.** Ask which program produced a red before diagnosing it.\n18. **A red that appears only locally is a claim about the invocation, not the branch**, and the reverse: a red that appears only in CI is a claim about the environment the check named.\n19. **Two workflows sharing a `name:` share `${{ github.workflow }}`.** Key concurrency on the filename; the check name in `gh pr checks` is the only way to tell which failed.\n20. **A gate that is configured is not a gate that runs.** Read a check's first ten lines for the early return before citing it as enforcing. **And before authorizing any write, enumerate the sibling guards that read the same artifact.**\n21. **A *successful* checkout is the more dangerous one.** It exits zero and moves a running session's HEAD silently. Tagging and merging need no working tree; a second lane gets a worktree.\n22. **A CLI message, error or success, is not a statement about state.** `gh pr view <n> --json mergeable,mergeStateStatus,reviewDecision,statusCheckRollup`; for merges, `--json state,mergedAt,mergeCommit`. **A `gh run list --limit 1` after a push returns the run that push started.** Select a run by the sha it ran on, never by \"latest.\"\n23. **A gate correct within its own field of view may not reach what it protects** (the seam class: a check that is right about its inputs and blind to the thing next to them).\n24. **The file in the tree is not the program the agent is running.**\n25. **A grep probe's tokenization is part of its claim.** Zero-hit results from an automated probe are re-checked by hand. **A carrier claim requires the executable line that reads the value.**\n26. **A reachability claim is only as good as its fetched history and its stated `merge-base --is-ancestor` argument order.**\n27. **A shallow marker in the shared `.git` lies to every session and subagent at once.** Check `git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository` first.\n28. **A register candidate is not a citable fact.** A finding number is citable only after the findings register holds it.\n29. **A signal field is a reading of its producer, not of the world.** Read the function that computed it.\n30. **A pipeline verdict and a definition-of-done verdict are two readers, not one state.**\n31. **An issue number recorded in the merged register is a repo read, not a forge read.**\n32. **A passing shape check is not evidence the value is usable.** **Before citing a check as proof a field is correct, read the code that consumes the field.**\n33. **An API error is not an empty result set.** **Every probe asserts its own success before its result is read**, never `.get()` into a default. And every probe in a chain guards its own value with `test -n` before the next command reads it; a null carried forward exits zero.\n34. **An identifier is not a reference until it resolves.** **Before writing an identifier into an artifact another session will act on, resolve it: to an issue, a sha, or a file path.**\n35. **A negative search inherits the scope of the corpus searched.** Absence in a subtree is not absence in the repository.\n36. **A check's answer is about a commit, not a PR.**\n37. **A gate's failure count is not its rejection count.**\n38. **A tracker search immediately after a create describes a moment before the create.** The number of a new issue is read from the URL its `create` printed, never from a search for its title.\n39. **A command block handed over for pasting contains no placeholder.** Either the value is derived inside the block, or the block is split so the value is read first and typed into the second.\n40. **`pytest --deselect` matches by nodeid prefix, not by exact string**, so a deselect line silently disables every test whose name extends the deselected one. The read that catches it is counting \"N deselected\" against the number of `--deselect` flags actually passed. The fix is renaming the new test, never widening the deselect.\n41. **An absence is a claim, and a claim needs its own read.** Reading a writer and its outputs never establishes that no declaration exists; only looking where a declaration would live does.\n42. **A fix that moves the fact to where the write is allowed, instead of moving the write to where the fact is known, is the pattern that made the pilot unusable.** Ask first: at what moment is this fact known, and what mechanism already stands there? Four tells, three of them countable: the fix adds a step, run, or commit to every item's traversal (the per-item counts in the records must not rise); it writes a fact after the event it describes, in a second commit; it ships with its own recovery path; its checks can reject nothing. The fourth is prose until the screen makes it a number: the fix has no consumer beyond this item, this tenant, this week. A one-time exception for the item in hand is how the pattern starts, and is refused on the same ground.\n\n**Git object reads against a local ref are trustworthy. Directory listings, anything remote, anything concurrent, and anything aggregated are not.**\n\n## The four the answer contract enforces first\n\n- **Enumerate the sibling guards.** Before authorizing any write, name every mechanism that reads the same artifact: the instruction that asks for it, the mechanism that performs it, the guard that judges it.\n- **Resolve every identifier.** An identifier is not a reference until it resolves to an issue, a sha, or a file.\n- **A gate's failure count is not its rejection count.** Read what was rejected and on how many distinct pieces of work before calling a gate earned.\n- **State the per-item cost of every fix, before and after.** Steps, runs, and commits per traversal, from the records. A fix whose after is larger than its before is not authorized without a ruling that names why (rule 42).\n# Counsel and the conversation: rulings of 2026-09-25\n\nObserved on #219 (R3's own build): attempt 1 halted after 3,510 s as `non-question`; `counsel-01` read it, verdict `answer`, text \"Continue the build from the existing working tree\"; under R2 the product filed an issue and waited for the operator. Under R3 counsel's `answer` is the next attempt's steer.\n\n## Troubleshoot, measured\n\nOne `attavo counsel 219 --ask \"why did this build stop?\"` in a scratch checkout: 73 s, three model turns. Turn one answered with no citations; the adapter's citation guard sent it back; it read the record, the last 30 KB of the 4.8 MB build log, searched for the brief and found nothing (the corpus was not in the repository); turn three answered with citations. It wrote `counsel-02.json` into the scratch checkout, which was discarded.\n\n## Ruled\n\n1. A question to counsel is a follow-up to a stop counsel has already read. `attavo counsel N --ask` is handed the prior counsel records for the item as its first input (verdict, text, citations), never re-derives a class a record states, and writes its answer as the next `counsel-NN.json` with `answers` naming the counsel record it follows beside `record`. The screen commits and pushes it like any counsel record. The answer is a record: an intervention in the attestation and precedent.\n2. The panel's first message is the newest counsel record read from the branch (no invocation); `--ask` runs only for a question the operator types.\n3. Resuming a vendor's own thread is an adapter-internal optimisation, recorded in the adapter's report when used, never part of the contract; the records are the mechanism that carries context between invocations.\n4. For a level that is the operator's (budget spent, two identical failures, counsel says `person`) the panel opens with the exhaustion report and ends in one of three drafted acts, each a record: a ruling (R4), a spec amendment (moves the approval), a product or tenant defect (counsel's `patch`).\n5. Counsel is handed the stop record, the signature and the stage's last events rather than the whole log, unless the question is about the log.\n6. Counsel's corpus, the text it reads before it answers (method rules, briefs, rulings), lives in the repository under `docs/`, named by `[counsel].reads`; a manifest naming a path that does not exist is refused.\n\n## The conversation and its records\n\nThe exchange is split from its outcome. A turn in the conversation is working material and lives in the product's datastore with the item; a record is written only for an outcome the attestation cares about: counsel's verdict on a stop (answer, patch, person); a follow-up whose answer changes what the next attempt is told; or an operator turning a turn into an act (a ruling, a spec amendment, an issue). The record carries the question that produced it. A question that belongs to Plan is answered and routed there; in Execute the only outcomes are those above, so a tangent produces nothing in git however long it runs.\n# R3 (retries, bounds and budgets): the nine rulings of 2026-09-25\n\nAll nine proposed by the session and ruled, agreed, by the operator on 2026-09-25 with the session's recommendations adopted. They sit on top of ruling C and its two amendments in the reconciler brief (2026-09-20). Item #219 carries the spec; v0.28.0 carries the code.\n\n1. **Failure signature.** `stop.signature` on every non-pass stop, compared by string equality, runs of digits removed on every family: last event line (died, hung); adapter's stop detail (transient, others); sorted names of red required checks (returned check); sorted titles of blocking findings (returned review). The exhaustion report prints signatures side by side; a signature too fine is the known failure mode and the side-by-side is how it is seen.\n2. **What a returned retry carries.** The red check's log via `[forge].check_log_command`, or the review's blocking findings. Collapsed with 3 into one input.\n3. **How a steer reaches the next attempt.** The existing `ATTAVO_FEEDBACK` file, seeded on the retried attempt's first iteration with class and signature, the evidence, and the newest steer newer than the blocking record (counsel's `answer` now, an operator's `/rule` after R4). The record carries `retry_of` and `steer`. No new adapter input.\n4. **Transient wait.** `[drive].retry_after_s`, default 300; died and hung retry on the next pass with no wait.\n5. **Ceilings.** `[drive.build].ceiling_s` and `[drive.verify].ceiling_s`, read against the corpus of build wall clocks at each pin; past it, terminated and recorded hung with `stop.bound = \"ceiling\"`; non-streaming adapters (`conformance.json` `streams_events: false`) are judged by the ceiling alone; the doctor refuses a `[drive]` table without both ceilings.\n6. **Budget accounting.** Every marker counts, recovered attempts included; the report always says attempt N of the budget.\n7. **`--again`.** Resets a spent budget only, recorded in the marker; a no-op inside the budget; retired in R4.\n8. **Environment refusal.** Recorded in R3, never retried per item; acted on by R4's tenant-wide breaker.\n9. **Exhaustion report's home.** `attavo drive N --report` (state stopped, `attempts`, `signatures_match`, `rulings_available`); no new command.\n\nFound while writing the spec: a review with findings passed whatever their severity (severity is free text); the returned-review family needed a definition, so a finding of severity `blocking` fails the review as `returned` and every other severity is filed as before. R2 as merged reads alive from the heartbeat and never calls a heartbeated worker hung by event age; the ceiling is therefore the only wall-clock bound a worker has, which is what ruling 5 intended.\n\nExclusions: no cross-executor retry; no backoff beyond the one fixed wait.\n# The three stores: where a thing lives (ruled 2026-09-25)\n\nOne test decides the store: does the attestation bind to it?\n\n1. **Git**: facts and policy the attestation binds to. The spec and its criteria; approval bound to the spec's sha; invocation markers; build, review, counsel and ruling records; the manifest, including policy such as `[operators].approvers` (a claim the attestation makes, so a tenant must be able to prove it with the repository alone). Small, versioned, travels with the branch, survives the product.\n2. **The product's datastore**: state and working material the attestation does not bind to. Identity state; indexes over git (ledger, measurements, report caches, legitimate only as caches rebuilt from git); the Plan area's working documents; the per-item conversation. A Plan document a spec's research record or a ruling cites is copied into `docs/` in the tenant at its cited version in the same commit as the citation.\n3. **Content-addressed bulk**: bytes a fact points at by hash: build logs, events files, later any executor evidence. The record carries `log_sha256` and a locator; the attestation binds to the hash. The backend is the tenant's declaration in the manifest, offered in this order: git LFS (keeps \"verify with the repository alone\" true), the tenant's own object store behind a `put`/`get` command pair, GitHub release assets. GitHub Actions artifacts are not a candidate: they expire.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Records are never moved to a datastore to save repository size; they are not the size.\n- First step: every build, verify and counsel record carries `log_sha256`, computed at write time, with the log still in git; one field that turns every later move into a change of locator, not of attestation. Beside it, two measurements at retire: repository size per item and log bytes per attempt; they say when to declare a bulk backend.\n- The index over git is built when a doctor or report read is slow enough to move a decision.\n- Counsel's corpus belongs in the repository under `docs/`; `[counsel].reads` names it.\n- Auth and RBAC: policy in git (the manifest), identity delegated to the forge, state in the datastore.\n{\n  \"sha\": \"9672d1ec4236c4543bac93b0e61a129ee9ab1b41\",\n  \"start_sha\": \"9672d1ec4236c4543bac93b0e61a129ee9ab1b41\",\n  \"spec_sha\": \"ae84b76712c95c91ad257aa5f0e4f44ca8ef1aae\",\n  \"approval_sha\": \"c46608c75c862887f345f7b99d9892e336f7d956\",\n  \"at\": \"2026-10-15T15:48:01Z\",\n  \"adapter\": \"claude-code\",\n  \"adapter_config\": {\n    \"auth\": \"subscription\",\n    \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-5\"\n  },\n  \"executor_identity\": {\n    \"name\": \"attavo executor (claude-code)\",\n    \"email\": \"executor+claude-code@attavo.invalid\"\n  },\n  \"reports\": [\n    \"tokens\",\n    \"cost_usd\"\n  ],\n  \"adapter_version\": \"2.1.295 (Claude Code)\",\n  \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-5\",\n  \"model_version\": \"claude-sonnet-5\",\n  \"iterations\": 2,\n  \"criteria\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"the-rejection-tests-exist.sh\",\n      \"exit\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"the-intervention-class-type-is-the-schemas-enum.sh\",\n      \"exit\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"selected_tests\": {\n    \"select\": \"python3 .attavo/bin/select-tests\",\n    \"run\": \"xargs -r python3 -m pytest -q -n ${ATTAVO_TEST_WORKERS:-auto} -m 'not own_records'\",\n    \"count\": 0,\n    \"exit\": 0\n  },\n  \"checks\": {\n    \"command\": \"python3 -m mypy\",\n    \"exit\": 1\n  },\n  \"tokens\": {\n    \"input\": 765150,\n    \"output\": 5720\n  },\n  \"cost_usd\": 0.3879404,\n  \"wall_clock_s\": 370.2,\n  \"status\": \"halted\",\n  \"refusal\": null,\n  \"halted\": {\n    \"question\": \"I'm trying to fix the mypy error left from the previous build attempt (attavo/attest.py:381: interventions typed as list[dict] but AttestRecord expects list[StatementIntervention]). The fix is to type attavo.ledger.item_acts_and_interventions's interventions return as list[StatementIntervention] and import that type into ledger.py. My Edit tool call to attavo/ledger.py is being blocked pending your permission approval \\u2014 can you approve it (or let me know if you'd rather I take a different approach)?\"\n  },\n  \"refusal_kind\": null,\n  \"report_class\": null,\n  \"retry_of\": \"build-01\",\n  \"steer\": null,\n  \"stop\": {\n    \"class\": \"question\",\n    \"provenance\": \"spec\",\n    \"detail\": \"I'm trying to fix the mypy error left from the previous build attempt (attavo/attest.py:381: interventions typed as list[dict] but AttestRecord expects list[StatementIntervention]). The fix is to type attavo.ledger.item_acts_and_interventions's interventions return as list[StatementIntervention] and import that type into ledger.py. My Edit tool call to attavo/ledger.py is being blocked pending your permission approval \\u2014 can you approve it (or let me know if you'd rather I take a different approach)?\",\n    \"signature\": \"I'm trying to fix the mypy error left from the previous build attempt (attavo/attest.py:: interventions typed as list[dict] but AttestRecord expects list[StatementIntervention]). The fix is to type attavo.ledger.item_acts_and_interventions's interventions return as list[StatementIntervention] and import that type into ledger.py. My Edit tool call to attavo/ledger.py is being blocked pending your permission approval \\u2014 can you approve it (or let me know if you'd rather I take a different approach)?\",\n    \"bound\": null\n  },\n  \"record_version\": 12,\n  \"item\": \"360\",\n  \"product_version\": \"v0.35.0-2-gb949cef\",\n  \"product_pin\": \"v0.35.0\"\n}\nschema_version = 1\n\n[attavo]\nversion = \"v0.35.0\"\n\n[forge]\nchecks_command = \"python3 .attavo/bin/forge-checks\"\ncheck_log_command = \"python3 .attavo/bin/forge-check-log\"\nfile_issue_command = \"python3 .attavo/bin/file-issue\"\nissues_command = \"python3 .attavo/bin/forge-issues\"\nattestation_command = \"sh .attavo/bin/forge-attestation\"\nsealed_since = \"83fcbc6863fdb65956b77768c66c2103d221521b\"\n\n[executor]\nbuild = \"claude-code\"\nverify = \"codex\"\n\n[executor.claude-code]\nauth = \"subscription\"\nmodel = \"claude-sonnet-5\"\n\n[executor.codex]\nmodel = \"gpt-5.6-sol\"\n\n[spec]\n[spec.boundary]\npaths = [\"bin/**\", \"attavo/**\", \"adapters/**\", \"schema/**\", \"templates/**\", \"action.yml\", \"scripts/**\"]\n\n[gates]\n[gates.spec]\ncommand = \"attavo spec-gate\"\nretires = [\n  \"the lane as the product's transport: a Claude Code lane session reporting to a reviewing session, with no spec, no approval bound to a commit, no build/verify separation, and no attestation on the product's own commits\",\n  \"the || true step in ci.yml that ran the doctor's four proofs against this repository expected to still fail\",\n  \"the reviewing session as the reader of whether the lane did what the issue said\",\n]\n\n[tests]\nselect = \"python3 .attavo/bin/select-tests\"\ncall_timeout_s = 240\nrun = \"xargs -r python3 -m pytest -q -n ${ATTAVO_TEST_WORKERS:-auto} -m 'not own_records'\"\nprogress = \"pytest\"\n\n[paths]\nspecs = \"attest/specs\"\nrecords = \"attest/records\"\nrulings = \"attest/rulings.md\"\nrejections = \"attest/rejections\"\n\n[doctor]\nbuilt_since = \"dae6d765f3d6\"\n\n[operators]\napprovers = [\"dbean10\"]\n\n[checks]\ncommand = \"python3 -m mypy\"\nprobe = \"python3 -c 'import pytest, xdist, mypy, jsonschema'\"\nprobe_timeout_s = 60\n\n[drive]\nstall_after_s = 1800\nattempts = 3\nretry_after_s = 300\n\n[drive.build]\nceiling_s = 3600\n\n[drive.verify]\nceiling_s = 1200\n\n[counsel]\nreads = [\"docs/method-rules.md\", \"docs/counsel-rulings.md\", \"docs/r3-rulings.md\", \"docs/storage-ruling.md\"]\n\n[cost.rates.\"gpt-5.6-sol\"]\ninput_usd_per_mtok = 5.0\noutput_usd_per_mtok = 30.0\nas_of = \"2026-10-06\"\nsource = \"https://openai.com/api/pricing/\"\n{\n  \"read\": true,\n  \"reason\": null,\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"doctor\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"success\",\n      \"run_url\": null,\n      \"log\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"schema-version\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"success\",\n      \"run_url\": null,\n      \"log\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"typecheck\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"failure\",\n      \"run_url\": null,\n      \"log\": \"/var/folders/k6/kwlls81j5gvf1p1_hbh9mn8m0000gn/T/tmp3644wusr/check-02.log\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"test\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"success\",\n      \"run_url\": null,\n      \"log\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"action-self-check\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"success\",\n      \"run_url\": null,\n      \"log\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"spec-gate\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"success\",\n      \"run_url\": null,\n      \"log\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"coherence\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"success\",\n      \"run_url\": null,\n      \"log\": null\n    }\n  ]\n}\n## the default branch, first-parent, since this item's branch point\n\n(nothing new)\n\n## this branch's own commits since the branch point\n\n9672d1ec4236c4543bac93b0e61a129ee9ab1b41 attavo driver attavo: build invocation 02 started for item 360\nb6fe84f92cd027c0e60d5e44a59dc0e7257c448a attavo driver attavo: build record for item 360 (build-01)\ne7c8d95c091bfffaffe6bce4d90e71d94ed929d8 attavo executor (claude-code) build for #360: four wave findings (clock-read count, put regression, exit-code order, intervention Literal)\n777498825b56655e82359d2bfa01897a1b083054 attavo driver attavo: build invocation 01 started for item 360\n29448a70f63b40748d7ea3e9d8ab1599a7974bc9 attavo-records[bot] attavo: approval record for item 360\nc46608c75c862887f345f7b99